{
  "id": "functions",
  "title": "Functions: Parabola, Hyperbola and Exponential",
  "term": 2,
  "summary": "Horizontal and vertical shifts of the parabola $y = a(x + p)^2 + q$, hyperbola $y = \\frac{a}{x + p} + q$ and exponential $y = ab^{x+p} + q$, turning points, asymptotes, axes of symmetry, and average gradient.",
  "notes": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Horizontal shifts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In Grade 11 we add a **horizontal shift** $p$. Replacing $x$ by $(x + p)$ moves a graph $p$ units to the **left** (if $p > 0$) or to the **right** (if $p < 0$)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The parabola $y = a(x + p)^2 + q$"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Turning point: $(-p; q)$.",
        "Axis of symmetry: $x = -p$.",
        "For $y = ax^2 + bx + c$: the axis of symmetry is $x = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}$; substitute to find the turning point.",
        "Range: $y \\geq q$ if $a > 0$; $y \\leq q$ if $a < 0$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Worked example 1",
      "problem": "Sketch $f(x) = x^2 - 2x - 3$. Give the turning point, intercepts and range.",
      "steps": [
        "Axis of symmetry: $x = -\\dfrac{-2}{2(1)} = 1$; $f(1) = -4$, so the turning point is $(1; -4)$.",
        "$y$-intercept: $(0; -3)$.",
        "$x$-intercepts: $(x - 3)(x + 1) = 0$: $(-1; 0)$ and $(3; 0)$.",
        "Range: $y \\geq -4$."
      ],
      "answer": "TP $(1; -4)$; intercepts $(0; -3)$, $(-1; 0)$, $(3; 0)$"
    },
