    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The hyperbola $y = \\dfrac{a}{x + p} + q$"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Vertical asymptote: $x = -p$. Horizontal asymptote: $y = q$.",
        "If $a > 0$ the branches are in the 1st and 3rd 'quadrants' formed by the asymptotes; if $a < 0$, in the 2nd and 4th.",
        "Axes of symmetry: $y = (x + p) + q$ and $y = -(x + p) + q$ (both pass through the point where the asymptotes meet).",
        "Domain: $x \\neq -p$. Range: $y \\neq q$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Worked example 2",
      "problem": "For $g(x) = \\dfrac{2}{x - 1} + 3$, give the asymptotes, $y$-intercept and $x$-intercept.",
      "steps": [
        "Asymptotes: $x = 1$ and $y = 3$.",
        "$y$-intercept: $g(0) = -2 + 3 = 1$: $(0; 1)$.",
        "$x$-intercept: $\\dfrac{2}{x - 1} = -3 \\Rightarrow x - 1 = -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\Rightarrow x = \\dfrac{1}{3}$."
      ],
      "answer": "Asymptotes $x = 1$, $y = 3$; intercepts $(0; 1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3}; 0)$"
